Q: Parsing Interval Notation to Guava Range A: <p>This issue has been around for quite a while, and it doesn't look like Google is planning to add native parsing support to Guava anytime soon.</p>
<p>I created a dedicated library to solve this problem: <a href="https://github.com/neewrobert/guava-range-parser" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/neewrobert/guava-range-parser</a></p>
<p>It supports all 9 Guava Range types with standard mathematical interval notation:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// Basic usage
Range&lt;Integer&gt; range = RangeParser.parse(&quot;[0..100)&quot;, Integer.class);
// Result: [0..100)

// Unbounded ranges
Range&lt;Integer&gt; atLeast = RangeParser.parse(&quot;[50..+∞)&quot;, Integer.class);
Range&lt;Integer&gt; all = RangeParser.parse(&quot;(-∞..+∞)&quot;, Integer.class);

// Works with various types
Range&lt;Double&gt; doubles = RangeParser.parse(&quot;[0.5..1.5)&quot;, Double.class);
Range&lt;LocalDate&gt; dates = RangeParser.parse(&quot;[2024-01-01..2024-12-31]&quot;, LocalDate.class);
Range&lt;Duration&gt; duration = RangeParser.parse(&quot;[PT1H..PT24H)&quot;, Duration.class);
</code></pre>
<pre><code>Maven Dependency:

&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;io.github.neewrobert&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;guava-range-parser-core&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;0.3.1&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The library also includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Spring Boot integration - automatic conversion for @ConfigurationProperties and @Value annotations</li>
<li>Jackson integration - JSON serialization/deserialization of Range objects</li>
<li>Built-in support for Integer, Long, Double, String, BigInteger, BigDecimal, LocalDate, LocalDateTime, Duration, and more</li>
</ul>
<p>Hope this helps anyone stumbling upon this issue! Also feel free to contribute if you want</p>

Q: Confused over event coordinates reported and calculated from JScrollPane A: <p>This answer addresses how to obtain the desired point in the view, but not what <code>toViewCoordinates</code> does. I am assuming my comments about this in your question hold, in order to continue.</p>
<p>There is no need to convert from pixel coordinates of the mouse event to view coordinates and back. We can leverage the fact that both the mouse event and the view are both pixel coordinate systems, so we only need to translate the point from the mouse source space to the target view space. Like so:</p>
<pre><code>Point pt = SwingUtilities.convertPoint(e.getComponent(), e.getPoint(), jPanel);
</code></pre>
<p><code>e</code> is the <code>MouseEvent</code> of the <code>JScrollPane</code> and the <code>pt</code> is now relative (in pixels) to the <code>jPanel</code> view.</p>
<p>Follows example code:</p>
<pre><code>import java.awt.Color;
import java.awt.Dimension;
import java.awt.Font;
import java.awt.Graphics;
import java.awt.MouseInfo;
import java.awt.Point;
import java.awt.event.MouseEvent;
import java.awt.event.MouseWheelEvent;
import javax.swing.JFrame;
import javax.swing.JPanel;
import javax.swing.JScrollPane;
import javax.swing.SwingUtilities;
import javax.swing.event.MouseInputAdapter;

public class MouseEventExample implements Runnable {

    private static String repeat(final String source,
                                 final int times) {
        final StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; times; ++i)
            sb.append(source);
        return sb.toString();
    }

    private static class DrawPanel extends JPanel {

        private final Point baselinePoint;

        public DrawPanel() {
            baselinePoint = new Point();
            super.setFont(new Font(Font.MONOSPACED, Font.BOLD, super.getFont().getSize()));
        }

        public void setBaselinePoint(final Point newBaselinePoint) {
            baselinePoint.setLocation(newBaselinePoint);
            repaint();
        }

        @Override
        protected void paintComponent(final Graphics g) {
            super.paintComponent(g);

            /*Create a string to draw in the view, in order to verify where the
            mouse points in the view:*/
            final String baselineString = String.format(&quot;baseline at (x=%d, y=%d)&quot;, baselinePoint.x, baselinePoint.y);

            /*Create an underline string with the same character length as
            'baselineString', in order to draw the actual point the mouse is
            pointing in the view, using an alternative (MouseInfo) method:*/
            final String underlineString = repeat(&quot;_&quot;, baselineString.length());

            //Obtain the screen location of the mouse:
            final Point actualMousePoint = MouseInfo.getPointerInfo().getLocation();

            /*Convert the screen location of the mouse to our draw panel's pixel
            coordinate system:*/
            SwingUtilities.convertPointFromScreen(actualMousePoint, this);

            /*Since we are using a monospaced font then both 'baselineString'
            and 'underlineString' will occupy the same pixel width, so drawing
            both will appear as if we are underlining the 'baselineString' if
            their baseline draw locations are the same...*/

            g.drawString(underlineString, actualMousePoint.x, actualMousePoint.y);
            g.setColor(Color.RED);
            g.drawString(baselineString, baselinePoint.x, baselinePoint.y);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(new MouseEventExample());
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {

        final DrawPanel drawPanel = new DrawPanel();
        drawPanel.setPreferredSize(new Dimension(1000, 1000));

        final JScrollPane pane = new JScrollPane(drawPanel);
        pane.getVerticalScrollBar().setUnitIncrement(20);
        pane.getHorizontalScrollBar().setUnitIncrement(20);

        final MouseInputAdapter locateMouseAdapter = new MouseInputAdapter() {
            private void locateMouseInView(final MouseEvent e) {
                //Both points are in pixels, so just translate from component to component:
                drawPanel.setBaselinePoint(SwingUtilities.convertPoint(e.getComponent(), e.getPoint(), drawPanel));
            }

            @Override public void mouseMoved(final MouseEvent e) { locateMouseInView(e); }
            @Override public void mouseDragged(final MouseEvent e) { locateMouseInView(e); }
            @Override public void mouseWheelMoved(final MouseWheelEvent e) { locateMouseInView(e); }
            @Override public void mouseExited(final MouseEvent e) { locateMouseInView(e); }
            @Override public void mouseEntered(final MouseEvent e) { locateMouseInView(e); }
        };
        pane.addMouseListener(locateMouseAdapter);
        pane.addMouseMotionListener(locateMouseAdapter);
        pane.addMouseWheelListener(locateMouseAdapter);

        /*The following call you are using sets the point of the view that will
        be visible in the upper left corner of the viewport. This works
        currently because viewport and view coordinate systems are the same
        (pixel coordinates). But in possible subclasses of viewport where these
        two systems are not the same, then the following call would have
        different results than the current one.*/
        pane.getViewport().setViewPosition(new Point(300, 300));

        final JFrame frame = new JFrame(&quot;MouseEventExample&quot;);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.add(pane);
        frame.setPreferredSize(new Dimension(300, 300));
        frame.pack();
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In the above example code, the <code>JScrollPane</code> tracks the mouse movements and converts its locations to panel space via <code>javax.swing.SwingUtilities#convertPoint</code> method. For each converted point a <code>String</code> is drawn in the panel, as if the point was the <code>Graphics</code> baseline. This, in effect, shows where the mouse is inside the panel (in panel coordinate system) and avoids any intermediate manual calculations.</p>
<p>In order to verify it works, we could draw another <code>String</code> at the <code>jPanel.getMousePosition()</code> position as @GilbertLeBlanc suggested in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79880225/confused-over-event-coordinates-reported-and-calculated-from-jscrollpane#comment140965482_79880225">their comment</a>. Alternatively we can directly use <code>java.awt.MouseInfo</code> and <code>javax.swing.SwingUtilities#convertPointFromScreen</code>. The latter method will never result in a <code>null</code> <code>Point</code> (as is the case of <code>getMousePosition</code>) and is used in the example code above.</p>
